      "topicId": "y12a-exam",
      "code": "3B",
      "title": "Stationary points and turning points",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "car2020s-q18",
          "type": "multipart",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "stem": "Consider the curve \\(y = 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 12x + 5\\).",
          "parts": [
            {
              "id": "car2020s-q18a",
              "type": "mc",
              "difficulty": "hard",
              "stem": "(a) Find the coordinates of the stationary points and determine their nature.",
              "options": [
                "Local maximum at \\((-1, 12)\\); local minimum at \\((2, -15)\\)",
                "Local minimum at \\((-1, 12)\\); local maximum at \\((2, -15)\\)",
                "Local maximum at \\((-2, 1)\\); local minimum at \\((1, -8)\\)",
                "Local minimum at \\((-2, 1)\\); local maximum at \\((1, -8)\\)"
              ],
              "answer": 0,
              "hint": "Find \\(y'\\) and set it to zero. Use \\(y''\\) to classify.",
              "solution": "The correct answer is Local maximum at \\((-1, 12)\\); local minimum at \\((2, -15)\\).",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "explain": "Step 1: Differentiate the function to find the first derivative \\(y'\\).",
                  "work": "$$ y' = 6x^2 - 6x - 12 $$"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "Step 2: Set \\(y' = 0\\) to find the \\(x\\)-coordinates of the stationary points.",
                  "work": "$$ \\begin{aligned} 6x^2 - 6x - 12 &= 0 \\\\\\\\ 6(x^2 - x - 2) &= 0 \\\\\\\\ (x - 2)(x + 1) &= 0 \\implies x = 2, x = -1 \\end{aligned} $$"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "Step 3: Find the corresponding \\(y\\)-coordinates by substituting \\(x = -1\\) and \\(x = 2\\) into the original equation.",
                  "work": "$$ \\begin{aligned} \\text{For } x = -1: &\\quad y = 2(-1)^3 - 3(-1)^2 - 12(-1) + 5 = 12 \\implies (-1, 12) \\\\\\\\ \\text{For } x = 2: &\\quad y = 2(2)^3 - 3(2)^2 - 12(2) + 5 = -15 \\implies (2, -15) \\end{aligned} $$"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "Step 4: Find the second derivative \\(y''\\) and use it to determine the nature of each stationary point.",
                  "work": "$$ \\begin{aligned} y'' &= 12x - 6 \\\\\\\\ \\text{At } x = -1: &\\quad y'' = 12(-1) - 6 = -18 < 0 \\implies \\text{Local Maximum at } (-1, 12) \\\\\\\\ \\text{At } x = 2: &\\quad y'' = 12(2) - 6 = 18 > 0 \\implies \\text{Local Minimum at } (2, -15) \\end{aligned} $$"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "car2020s-q18b",
              "type": "mc",
              "difficulty": "hard",
              "stem": "(b) Which of the following is true about its point of inflection?",
              "options": [
                "It occurs at \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) because \\(y'' = 0\\) and concavity changes.",
                "It occurs at \\(x = 1\\) because \\(y' = 0\\) and gradient changes.",
                "It occurs at \\(x = -1\\) because \\(y'' = 0\\) and concavity changes.",
                "There is no point of inflection."
              ],
              "answer": 0,
              "hint": "Set \\(y'' = 0\\) and verify that the sign of \\(y''\\) changes.",
              "solution": "The correct answer is It occurs at \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) because \\(y'' = 0\\) and concavity changes.",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "explain": "Step 1: Set the second derivative \\(y''\\) to zero to find the possible point of inflection.",
                  "work": "$$ \\begin{aligned} y'' &= 12x - 6 \\\\\\\\ 12x - 6 &= 0 \\implies 12x = 6 \\implies x = \\frac{1}{2} \\end{aligned} $$"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "Step 2: Find the corresponding \\(y\\)-coordinate by substituting \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) into the original equation.",
                  "work": "$$ \\text{For } x = \\frac{1}{2}: \\quad y = 2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3 - 3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 - 12\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) + 5 = -1.5 \\implies \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, -1.5\\right) $$"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "Step 3: Verify that the concavity actually changes at \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) by testing \\(y''\\) on either side.",
                  "work": "$$ \\begin{aligned} \\text{Test } x = 0: &\\quad y''(0) = -6 < 0 \\quad (\\text{Concave down}) \\\\\\\\ \\text{Test } x = 1: &\\quad y''(1) = 6 > 0 \\quad (\\text{Concave up}) \\end{aligned} $$"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "Step 4: Conclude that a change in concavity confirms a point of inflection.",
                  "work": "$$ \\text{Since concavity changes, there is a true point of inflection at } \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, -1.5\\right). $$"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "car2020s-q18c",
              "type": "review",
              "difficulty": "hard",
